  "image_name": "2a89f033ac2caf5d4640be8d75a4892d.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure illustrates two-way communication between perivascular adipose tissue (PVAT) and the arterial wall during atherosclerosis-like vascular injury. The artery is shown in layers, with the lumen at the top, then intima, media, adventitia, and PVAT surrounding the vessel from the outside. In the intima, a plaque contains foam cells, apoptotic cells, inflammatory cells, and a necrotic core, indicating advanced lesion development with endothelial activation and dysfunction.\n\nThe key message is that signals move in both directions. “Outside-to-inside” signaling originates in inflamed PVAT, where adipocytes and inflammatory macrophages release adipokines, lipid mediators, reactive oxygen species, and other inflammatory factors that appear to penetrate inward through the vessel wall and contribute to endothelial dysfunction and plaque progression. Conversely, “inside-to-outside” signaling comes from the diseased plaque, which produces cytokines, ROS, and 4-HNE that seem to spread outward toward PVAT and promote PVAT inflammation.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes a vicious cycle: vascular inflammation and oxidative stress in the plaque stimulate inflammation in surrounding fat, and inflamed PVAT feeds back to worsen the arterial lesion.",
